<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sd1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>SD1: Solutions to Differential Equations</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can use matrix algebra to compute linear combinations, products of matrices, and determinants of matrices, as well as determine when these are not possible.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <subsection xml:id="sec-matrices">
        <title>Matrices</title>
        
        <definition xml:id="def-matrix">
            <title>Matrix</title>
            <idx><h>matrix (definition)</h></idx>
            <idx><h>matrix (square)</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A <term>matrix</term> is an array of the form 
                    <md>
                        \begin{pmatrix}
                            a_{11} \amp a_{12} \amp \cdots \amp a_{1n} \\
                            a_{21} \amp a_{22} \amp \cdots \amp a_{2n} \\
                            \vdots \amp \vdots \amp \ddots \amp \cdots \\
                            a_{m1} \amp a_{m2} \amp \cdots \amp a_{mn} \\
                        \end{pmatrix}
                    </md>
                    where <m>m</m> represents the number of <em>rows</em> in the matrix and <m>n</m> represents the number of <em>columns</em> in the matrix. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    If <m>m = n</m>, i.e. the number of rows and columns are the same, then we say matrix is a <term>square matrix</term>. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An example of a <m>3\times 4</m> matrix is given by <m>\begin{pmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 7 \amp \dfrac{1}{7} \amp e \amp \pi \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>
    </subsection>

